lar Congestion**\n    *   **Trend: Worsening (with subsequent improvement)**\n    *   **Details:** Not mentioned in Series 1. Present in Series 2, highly indicative of pulmonary vascular congestion secondary to cardiac decompensation. Not noted in Series 3, suggesting resolution along with the improvement in cardiac status.\n\n## Forecasted Evolution\nWithout appropriate diagnosis and treatment, particularly for the underlying interstitial lung disease and potential cardiac issues, recurrent episodes of cardiac decompensation (pulmonary edema, effusions) are likely. The interstitial lung disease may progress, leading to increased respiratory morbidity.\n\n## Treatment Recommendations\n*   Given the persistent interstitial pattern, a high-resolution CT (HRCT) of the chest is strongly recommended to better characterize the interstitial lung disease and guide diagnosis.\n*   Comprehensive cardiovascular evaluation is warranted to investigate the cause of the cardiomegaly and episodes of pulmonary edema/effusions.\n*   Serial pulmonary function testing may be beneficial to monitor lung function over time.\n*   Regular clinical follow-up and consideration of further imaging are advised to monitor both cardiac and pulmonary statuses."
